{
  "raw": {
    "https://anselm.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Anselm/job/Saint-Anselm-College-Campus/Head-Coach--Women-s-Flag-Football_R1701": {
      "title": "Head Coach, Women's Flag Football",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-13\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nPosition summary Saint Anselm College, a private, liberal arts college in Benedictine tradition, is seeking a full-time, year-round Head Women’s Flag Football Coach.  Reporting to the Deputy Athletic Director for External Relations, the Head Women’s Flag Football Coach will establish and lead Saint Anselm College’s women’s flag football program. The coach will build a program that supports the College’s Catholic, Benedictine mission and provides student-athletes with a strong academic, personal, and athletic experience.   The first-year priority is recruiting an inaugural roster of at least 15-20 enrolled student-athletes for fall 2027. The coach will also develop the program’s culture, prepare for its first season, and work with Athletics leadership on a competition schedule and operating budget for separate approval. As the program becomes established, the coach will assume the full range of head coaching responsibilities and may receive additional Athletics Department duties beginning in year two.   First-year recruiting responsibilities Develop a written recruiting plan within 30 days of hire. The plan will identify target schools and events, a travel and outreach calendar, coordination with Admissions, and a prospect pipeline designed to support the 15-20 player roster goal. Focus in-person recruiting on New Hampshire and Massachusetts, including Greater Boston, the North Shore, and the Merrimack Valley. Make targeted visits to Rhode Island, Connecticut, New Jersey, and New York when the number of prospects and quality of an event justify the travel. Recruit in other markets through video and remote outreach, with additional travel subject to approval. Build relationships with high school, club, NFL FLAG, and community coaches. Identify prospective students Coordinate approved campus visits and prospect events with Admissions and other College offices. Maintain accurate recruiting records. Provide the Deputy Director of Athletics and Admissions with a monthly report showing qualified prospects, applications, admits, deposits, enrolled students, and projected roster composition. Review progress and adjust the recruiting plan each month. Represent the College accurately in all recruiting conversations and coordinate admissions and financial aid messaging through the appropriate College offices.   Program leadership and coaching responsibilities Direct all phases of the women’s flag football program, including recruiting, roster development, instruction, practice planning, competition preparation, student-athlete development, and program administration. Establish team standards that promote academic success, retention, graduation, sportsmanship, personal development, community engagement, and respect for others. Teach sound techniques and conduct practices and contests in a manner that prioritizes student-athlete health and safety. Coordinate conditioning, injury prevention, and return-to-play matters with athletic training and strength and conditioning staff. Work with Athletics leadership to propose the spring 2028 operating budget, equipment needs, facility requirements, staffing plan, and competition schedule. Manage approved funds, travel, purchases, and records in accordance with College procedures. Recruit, recommend, supervise, and evaluate assistant coaches and student personnel when those positions are approved. Maintain a culture of compliance within the program and follow applicable NCAA, conference, sport-governing-body, and College policies. Complete required administrative and compliance documentation. Work with Athletics communications, development, and other College offices on appropriate program promotion, alumni outreach, community engagement, and approved fundraising activities. Communicate professionally with student-athletes, families, College personnel, prospective students, coaches, and external partners. Attend department and campus events as assigned and perform other duties as assigned by the Director of Athletics. Additional department-wide responsibilities may be assigned beginning in year two, with consideration for the program’s coaching, recruiting, and student-athlete needs.   Qualifications   Required Bachelor’s degree. Demonstrated experience coaching or leading flag football. Ability to identify and recruit students who can succeed at Saint Anselm. Strong teaching, communication, organizational, and relationship-building skills. Commitment to student-athlete welfare, academic success, and an inclusive learning environment. Ability to travel and work evenings and weekends as the position requires. Ability to meet College employment requirements.   Preferred Collegiate coaching or recruiting experience. Experience launching or growing a team or athletic program. Familiarity with NCAA Division III rules and the developing collegiate women’s flag football landscape. Recruiting relationships in New England, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, New York and Mid-Atlantic region.     Successful candidates will be able to assist the college in advancing its Catholic identity and strategic priorities, which include institution-wide diversity and inclusiveness."
    },
    "https://anselm.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Anselm/job/Saint-Anselm-College-Campus/Dean-of-the-School-of-Arts-and-Sciences_R1712": {
      "title": "Dean of the School of Arts and Sciences",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-13\nPosted: Posted 2 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSaint Anselm College invites qualified applicants to apply for the position of the inaugural Dean of the School of Arts and Sciences. Alongside the established Jean School of Nursing and the new Robert J. Grappone School of Business and Innovation, the new School of Arts and Sciences will be grounded in the College’s Catholic, Benedictine, liberal arts identity.   Through broad engagement with the arts, humanities, natural sciences, and social sciences, the School of Arts and Sciences will foster a robust research environment that provides an education that is rigorous, personalized, and transformational, with learning opportunities at the local, national and global levels. Its graduates will be prepared to contribute meaningfully to diverse professions and communities, while advancing knowledge across disciplines.   The School of Arts and Sciences will emphasize the distinctive contributions of its departments including Biological Sciences, Chemistry and Forensics, Criminal Justice, Education, English and Communication, Fine Arts, History and Classics, Modern Languages, Philosophy, Physics, Politics, Psychology, Sociology and Social Work, and Theology.  The Dean of Arts and Sciences will champion the departments within the school, while overseeing the internal administrative structures and processes that advance the School’s academic mission.   The Dean of Arts and Sciences will provide visionary leadership for the liberal arts, positioning the school as a defining hallmark of the College, advancing academic excellence, innovation, research, scholarship and interdisciplinary collaboration. The Dean will shape and execute the school’s vision, mission, and strategic priorities, advance academic outcomes and student competencies, and build a culture of collaboration and innovation. This position is an exciting opportunity to strengthen and advance a distinctive liberal arts education through strategic partnerships, fundraising, and marketing and enrollment initiatives.    The successful candidate will be a collaborative and strategic leader with strong interpersonal skills and a demonstrated record of advancing academic programs and cultivating meaningful partnerships with a variety of community and educational stakeholders. The Dean will be responsible for ensuring compliance with regulatory and accrediting bodies, including NECHE. Consistent with College policies and procedures, the Dean will report directly to the Vice President for Academic Affairs. As the Inaugural Dean, the successful candidate will play a vital role in advancing the College’s mission and strengthening its liberal arts, Catholic, and Benedictine identity, while fostering an inclusive environment in which people of all faiths are welcomed.   Inclusivity Statement   In order to realize our mission as a Catholic, Benedictine, liberal arts college, Saint Anselm College is committed to sustaining an intentionally inclusive environment that will benefit all constituencies. In particular, the college is committed to providing students with the richest opportunities to learn, both in the classroom and within the environment of the college community as a whole. We accomplish this, in part, by fostering understanding, experience, and appreciation of the world's diversity through the academic curriculum, with its spirit of scholarly inquiry and respectful discourse; through a variety of service, co-curricular, religious, and academic activities and support mechanisms; and through the different perspectives and backgrounds of the members of our community. Committed to our Catholic mission, Saint Anselm College aspires to bring together a faculty, staff, and student body that enhances this educational endeavor with diverse scholarly interests, ideas, beliefs, experience, talents, and racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic backgrounds.   Qualifications   Ph.D. or other terminal degree in an academic discipline within the arts, humanities, social sciences, natural sciences, or a related field; Significant experience in undergraduate and graduate liberal arts related education; Substantial teaching experience, with knowledge surrounding pedagogy and online course delivery, including the delivery of advanced practice programs; Academic or administrative leadership experience, including strategic plan development and implementation; Demonstrated interest in and appreciation for the liberal arts; Commitment to academic freedom; and Eligibility for appointment to a tenured position in an academic discipline within the proposed School of Arts and Sciences.   Initial Priorities  Engage in strategic plan development and implementation in support of the School of Arts and Sciences and in alignment with the strategic plan of the College. This includes ensuring initiatives are properly staffed and resourced and working collaboratively with various College constituencies in support of new and ongoing programming; Foster an inclusive environment which promotes engagement with a diverse student body; Act as a primary representative with all internal and external constituencies associated with the School. Focus on relationship building between current and future faculty and administrators to strengthen community ties and work toward partnerships promoting experiential learning for students; Collaborate with the college’s Advancement team and execute a targeted fundraising and engagement plan for the School; Collaborate with the Office of College Communications and Marketing as well as the Office of Enrollment to recruit and enroll academically qualified, talented students, including students from diverse backgrounds; Collaborate with the Office of Partnerships to build strong and sustainable partnerships with a variety of community and educational stakeholders; Support faculty and program leaders in the assessment, revision, and development of curriculum; Develop staffing and budget plans for the School of Arts and Sciences Represent the School on College committees, as well as other groups designated by the President or Vice President for Academic Affairs; and Work collaboratively with central academic leadership, Faculty Senate, and Administration to further the goals of the School and the College.   Programmatic Details The Dean of the School of Arts and Sciences will:   Supervise School department chairs and program leaders; those who will report directly to the Dean include the department chairs and program directors for all departments in the School; graduate program directors; directors of the core curriculum, the Enduring Questions first-year program, and the College’s Writing Program; and the directors of centers and institutes, including the Gregory J. Grappone Humanities Institute, the Institute for Saint Anselm Studies, the Center for Ethics in Society, the Center for the Study of Religion and Public Life, and the Chapel Art Center.  The Dean and the leadership of liberal arts and other related programs will work collaboratively on existing and new programs;   Work collaboratively with key stakeholders to hire, evaluate, and retain qualified faculty, adjunct faculty, clinical faculty, and staff; Ensure program effectiveness through ongoing curriculum evaluation; Assess new programs which meet the needs of the community of interest and complement the mission and goals of the School of Arts and Sciences; Represent the School, or arrange for representation through a designee, at on- and off-campus events including, but not limited to: New Student and Accepted Student Days, Alumni meetings, Honors Convocation, Commencement, and various community and College events; Convene ad hoc committees of faculty, staff, and/or students necessary to the administration of the School of Arts and Sciences; Support faculty through participation in the promotion and tenure process; and Promote interdisciplinary and interprofessional collaboration within the School and College.   Accreditation Assure compliance with regulatory requirements including NECHE and other accreditation bodies.   Budget Work closely with the Finance Office and the Vice President for Academic Affairs on all matters pertaining to the school budget; and Maintain accountability for a balanced school budget.   Fundraising Work in conjunction with the Office of Advancement to lead efforts for external fundraising from philanthropic organizations/individuals; Actively pursue grants and other outside funding for major projects and equipment; and Identify sources to support student research, faculty research, and scholarship.      Partnerships Expand the community engagement of the School of Arts and Sciences by fostering current and new partnerships.     About Saint Anselm College   Saint Anselm College was founded in 1889 by the Benedictine monks of St. Mary’s Abbey of Newark, New Jersey, in response to the invitation by Bishop Denis M. Bradley, the first bishop of Manchester, New Hampshire. Since the College’s founding, a monastic community has resided on-campus and the Benedictine monks of Saint Anselm are an integral part of the College and its tradition. As a community of 31 monks, composed of priests and brothers, Saint Anselm Abbey is one of the 22 houses of the American Cassinese Congregation of Benedictines, members of the Order of Saint Benedict (O.S.B.).   Over the past quarter century, the College has dramatically grown as a residential liberal arts college, with more than 88 percent of students now residing on campus. The entering class in the fall of 2025 was the largest in the College’s history.   Following the launch of the Jean School of Nursing and Health Sciences in 2023, two new schools, the Robert J. Grappone School of Business and Innovation and the School of Arts and Sciences, will launch in 2027, completing an academic reorganization to a school-based structure. To support this growth, the College has worked to update and expand its facilities. Most recently, a new 40,000 square foot facility, Grappone Hall, opened in 2025 to house the School of Nursing and Health Sciences.   Other recent capital projects at the College include the 2025 conversion of Joseph Hall to a residence hall to accommodate a growing student population, the opening of the new home of the Gregory J. Grappone ’04 Humanities Institute in 2023, the construction of the Savard Welcome Center, which opened in the summer of 2020 and serves as the front porch of the College in the Benedictine expression of hospitality, and the opening of the new Roger and Francine Jean Student Center Complex in 2018.      College Mission   Saint Anselm College is a Catholic, Benedictine institution providing all its students a distinctive liberal arts education that incorporates opportunities for professional and career preparation. It does so in a learning community that encourages the lifelong pursuit of the truth and fosters intellectual, moral and spiritual growth to sustain and enrich its graduates’ personal lives, work, and engagement within local, national, and global communities. For more than 130 years, Saint Anselm College has remained committed to its mission and tradition, constantly building on its strengths and evolving to meet the changing needs of its current and prospective students. What makes an Anselmian education distinctive and exceptional are students’ deep involvement and integrative growth guided by a Benedictine-inspired educational philosophy.   Manchester, New Hampshire   Manchester is one hour from Boston, one hour from the New Hampshire seacoast, one hour from New Hampshire’s Lakes Region and White Mountains and is a dynamic and growing city that combines exciting urban opportunities with nearby natural splendor. As the largest city in New Hampshire, Manchester is home to leading cultural attractions including the Currier Museum of Art, the Palace Theatre, and the Historic Millyard. With many restaurants, a minor league baseball team, an airport, and an entertainment arena, there is no shortage of things to occupy one’s time. Successful candidates will be able to assist the college in advancing its Catholic identity and strategic priorities, which include institution-wide diversity and inclusiveness."
    },
    "https://anselm.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Anselm/job/Saint-Anselm-College-Campus/Coordinator--Clinical-Placements-and-Partnerships_R1711": {
      "title": "Coordinator, Clinical Placements and Partnerships",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-08\nPosted: Posted 7 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSaint Anselm College, a private, liberal arts college in the Benedictine tradition, is seeking a Coordinator, Clinical Placements and Partnerships for the Jean School of Nursing and Health Sciences.  This position coordinates clinical placements, experiential learning internships, and related activities for faculty and students across the Jean School of Nursing and Health Sciences. The Coordinator develops and maintains strong partnerships with healthcare and public health organizations to support high-quality student learning experiences. The position also collaborates closely with the Jean School Dean and Program Directors to assist with programmatic, operational and administrative activities in support of Jean School programs. The Coordinator, Clinical Placements and Partnerships is a 12-month calendar year position and reports directly to the Dean of the Jean School. Responsibilities: Clinical Rotation Assignments and Placements Coordinate clinical rotations, specialty experiences, observations, and undergraduate preceptorships across fall, winter, spring, and summer semesters. Coordinate student and faculty onboarding requirements for clinical placements, including health, safety, facility, EMR, and other site-specific requirements. Communicate clinical expectations and onboarding responsibilities to students and faculty. Undergraduate Preceptorship Placements Coordinate the undergraduate preceptorship application and placement process, including presentations, application timelines, resume and cover letter preparation, student advising, and placement submissions. Develop and maintain placement tracking systems and submit required documentation through the Centralized Clinical Placement (CCP) system and to external clinical partners. Ensure compliance with applicable state Board of Nursing requirements. Communicate placement assignments, deadlines, documentation, and onboarding requirements to students. Experiential Learning Internships Coordinate student experiential learning experiences with the Community & Public Health Program Director Submit required documentation through the Centralized Clinical Placement (CCP) system and to external partners. Ensure compliance with applicable institutional partner requirements. Communicate placement assignments, deadlines, documentation, and onboarding requirements to students. Health Requirements and Compliance Coordinate and monitor student compliance with drug screening, background checks, immunizations, BLS certification, FIT testing, and other clinical requirements. Maintain and monitor student compliance through CastleBranch and ensure accurate documentation of exemptions and required health records. Update health and immunization requirements in accordance with current CDC recommendations and facility requirements. Serve as a resource for students regarding health, immunization, and clinical compliance requirements. Coordinate onboarding and compliance requirements for Health Sciences internship students as assigned. Healthcare Partnerships and Affiliation Agreements Develop, establish, and maintain relationships with healthcare and public health organizations that provide clinical and internship experiences. Serve as the Jean School of Nursing and Health Sciences liaison to clinical partners and healthcare agencies. Initiate and coordinate affiliation agreements and contracts in collaboration with the Director. Seek new clinical opportunities and represent the nursing program at regional nursing and clinical placement meetings. Centralized Clinical Placement (CCP) Manage the Jean School's CCP account, including requesting, renewing, and updating clinical and preceptorship placements. Maintain accurate faculty, student, clinical site, schedule, and placement information within CCP and for non-CCP sites. Participate in regional CCP meetings and coordinate the annual CCP agreement. Student Support and Program Administration Collaborate with students, faculty, Health Services, Accessibility Resource Center, Athletics, Registrar, Career Development, and other College departments to address clinical placement, accommodation, and student support needs. Coordinate modified or alternative clinical placements in collaboration with appropriate College personnel and healthcare organizations. Assist with adjunct faculty and staff recruitment and onboarding activities. Respond to student, faculty, and healthcare organization inquiries and assist the Dean and Program Directors with additional programmatic and administrative responsibilities. Preferred Qualifications and Skills Bachelor’s degree preferred; degree in nursing, healthcare, education, business, or a related field is desirable. Prior experience coordinating clinical, internship, or experiential learning placements required. Proficiency with Google Workspace and Microsoft Office; willingness and ability to learn and utilize additional technologies, including Centralized Clinical Placement (CCP), CastleBranch, and Canvas. Demonstrated ability to manage multiple priorities, projects, and deadlines in a fast-paced, highly collaborative environment. Familiarity with student affiliation agreements, healthcare partnerships, and contract processes preferred. Excellent interpersonal, written and verbal communication, organizational, and customer service skills. Demonstrated ability to build and maintain effective relationships with students, faculty, healthcare partners, and internal College departments. Successful candidates will be able to assist the college in advancing its Catholic identity and strategic priorities, which include institution-wide diversity and inclusiveness."
    },
    "https://anselm.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Anselm/job/Saint-Anselm-College-Campus/Prevention-Education-Specialist_R1710": {
      "title": "Prevention Education Specialist",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-10-01\nPosted: Posted 14 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSaint Anselm College, a private, liberal arts college in Benedictine tradition, is seeking a full-time, 11-month (June off) Prevention Education Specialist. The Prevention Education Specialist develops and implements campus-wide violence prevention education while serving as one of the College's designated Confidential Resource Advisors (CRA) for students impacted by sexual misconduct, relationship violence, domestic violence, stalking, and sexual harassment. Rooted in Saint Anselm College's Benedictine tradition, this position promotes a campus culture of hospitality, community, respect for the inherent dignity of every person, and care for the common good through evidence-informed prevention initiatives, outreach, and education. As one of the College's designated Confidential Resource Advisors, the Prevention Education Specialist provides confidential support, information, safety planning, and referrals, but does not conduct investigations or participate in disciplinary decision-making.   The position reports to the Director of Operations at The Harbor, the violence prevention office within Saint Anselm College Health Services, and collaborates with Student Affairs, Title IX, Athletics, Residence Life, Campus Safety, Counseling, faculty, staff, and community partners.   This position is supported by grant funding and is subject to the continued availability of funding.   Essential Duties and Responsibilities Prevention Education Develop, implement, and evaluate campus-wide violence prevention programs that foster respect, inclusion, belonging, and personal responsibility. Facilitate workshops on consent, healthy relationships, bystander intervention, sexual violence, dating and domestic violence, stalking, trauma-informed practices, and related topics. Coordinate awareness campaigns and outreach events that strengthen community and promote student well-being. Recruit and support peer educators and student leaders. Assess program effectiveness and maintain participation data.   Confidential Resource Services Serve as one of the College's designated Confidential Resource Advisor for students affected by interpersonal violence. Provide trauma-informed support, crisis intervention, safety planning, and resource referrals in a manner that honors each individual's dignity, autonomy, and lived experience. Explain reporting options, supportive measures, institutional processes, and community resources while respecting student choice. Accompany students to meetings or appointments, as appropriate. Maintain confidentiality in accordance with New Hampshire law and institutional policy.   Collaboration & Compliance Build collaborative relationships across campus and with community partners to advance a coordinated, compassionate response to interpersonal violence. Support compliance with the Clery Act, VAWA, Title IX education requirements, and applicable New Hampshire laws. Assist with grant-funded initiatives, assessment, and reporting.   Minimum Qualifications Bachelor's degree in psychology, social work, counseling, public health, criminal justice, education, or a related field. Two years of experience in prevention education, higher education, victim services, counseling, public health, or a related field. Knowledge of trauma-informed practices and interpersonal violence prevention. Strong presentation, communication, and organizational skills.   Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education. Knowledge of Title IX, the Clery Act, VAWA, and New Hampshire Confidential Resource Advisor laws. Experience with grant-funded programs and program evaluation.   Successful candidates will be able to assist the college in advancing its Catholic identity and strategic priorities, which include institution-wide diversity and inclusiveness."
    },
    "https://anselm.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Anselm/job/Saint-Anselm-College-Campus/Clinical-Nursing-Faculty--Jean-School-of-Nursing-and-Health-Sciences--Medical-Surgical-Nursing-_R1700": {
      "title": "Clinical Nursing Faculty, Jean School of Nursing and Health Sciences (Medical Surgical Nursing)",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nThe Jean School of Nursing and Health Sciences at Saint Anselm College invites applications for a full-time Clinical Faculty position in medical surgical nursing to begin in January, 2027. The successful candidate will teach nursing students both in the classroom and clinical setting.  Interested applicants should submit a letter of interest and curriculum vitae via the college website to the attention of Dr. Carrie E. MacLeod, Director of Undergraduate Nursing.  Prior to interview, three letters of recommendation and academic transcripts will be required.  Applications will be reviewed as they are received and until the position is filled.      Required Skills:   A Master’s degree is required, a Ph.D. is preferred. Ph.D. prepared applicants, or applicants enrolled in a doctoral program, may be considered for a tenure-track position. All candidates should have nursing education teaching experience, and demonstrate clinical expertise in the care of the adult patient.  Candidates should also demonstrate a strong commitment to excellence in teaching at the undergraduate level.   Successful candidates will be able to assist the college in advancing its mission and strategic priorities , which includes institution-wide diversity and inclusiveness .   Come join a team that is preparing future nursing and health care leaders for successful career and life outcomes.  At Saint Anselm College, students develop critical-thinking skills, ethical decision-making, and a humanistic approach to patient care and community challenges.   Founded in 1889, Saint Anselm College is a four-year liberal arts college providing a 21st century education in the Catholic, Benedictine tradition. Located in the   greater Boston region in southern New Hampshire, Saint Anselm is well known for its strong liberal arts curriculum, the New Hampshire Institute of Politics, commitment to inclusiveness, a highly successful nursing program,   and a legacy of community service."
    },
    "https://anselm.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Anselm/job/Saint-Anselm-College-Campus/Accounts-Payable-Manager_R1699": {
      "title": "Accounts Payable Manager",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-29\nPosted: Posted 16 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSaint Anselm College, a Catholic liberal arts college in the Benedictine tradition, invites applications for the position of Accounts Payable Manager. Reporting to the Controller, the Accounts Payable Manager is responsible for the accurate and timely processing of accounts payable transactions and employee expense reimbursements while providing excellent customer service to faculty, staff, students, and suppliers The ideal candidate is detail-oriented, highly organized, collaborative, and comfortable working in a fast-paced higher education environment. This position plays a key role in maintaining strong internal controls and supporting the College’s financial operations through the effective use of Workday. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Process invoices, check requests, and payment transactions in a timely and accurate manner using Workday. Ensure proper approvals, coding, and supporting documentation for all disbursements Review and process employee expense reports in accordance with College policies and IRS regulations. Coordinate with purchasing to maintain supplier records, including W-9 documentation and payment setup. Reconcile year-end accounts payable data to accurately prepare IRS Form 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC Reconcile accounts payable transactions and resolve invoice discrepancies or payment issues. Assist with month-end and year-end closing activities related to accounts payable. Prepare and maintain prepaid expense schedules and monthly amortization entries. Support fixed asset accounting, including asset additions, disposals, transfers, depreciation tracking, and inventory maintenance. Ensure compliance with internal controls, accounting standards, and institutional policies. Respond to vendor and campus community inquiries professionally and promptly. Assist with audits by providing requested documentation and reports. Generate reports and support process improvements for supplier invoice and expense processes within Workday and related financial systems. Responsible for tracking uncleared checks and reporting Abandoned Property to the State of New Hampshire. Perform other related duties as assigned. Qualifications Associate’s or Bachelor’s degree in Accounting, Finance, Business Administration, or related field preferred. Minimum of 2 years of accounts payable or general accounting experience required. Experience using Workday financial systems strongly preferred. Knowledge of accounts payable processes, expense reimbursements, prepaid expenses, and fixed asset accounting. Strong attention to detail and organizational skills. Ability to manage multiple priorities and meet deadlines in a fast-paced environment. Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills. Proficiency in Microsoft Excel and other Microsoft Office applications. Commitment to confidentiality and high ethical standards.   Preferred Qualifications Experience in higher education or nonprofit accounting. Familiarity with month-end close processes and audit support. Familiarity with electronic invoice and workflow systems   In order to be considered for this position, please submit both a resume and a cover letter. Successful candidates will be able to assist the college to further its strategic goals for institution-wide diversity and inclusiveness ."
    },
    "https://anselm.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Anselm/job/Saint-Anselm-College-Campus/Deputy-Director--New-Hampshire-Institute-of-Politics_R1682": {
      "title": "Deputy Director, New Hampshire Institute of Politics",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-28\nPosted: Posted 17 Days Ago\nFull time\n\nSaint Anselm College, a private, liberal arts college in the Benedictine tradition, is seeking a dynamic Deputy Director for the New Hampshire Institute of Politics (NHIOP).  Reporting to the Executive Director of the NHIOP, the Deputy Director oversees the strategic, financial, supervisory, communications, and institutional leadership responsibilities. Programming, Communication and External Relations Identify strategic programming and funding opportunities Manage the overall planning, coordination and oversight of Institute programming including program selections to ensure quality and consistency while considering Institute priorities and coordinating with campus and community partners Oversee the Institute's public relations, branding, and communications, including press releases, Institute speeches, and newsletters while maintaining quality, tone, and consistency Manage speaker, special guests and donor recruitment, invitations, and correspondence to ensure a continued partnership and a positive guest experience Build and maintain relationships with authors, journalists, elected officials, publishers, community partners, special guests and vendors Coordinate the publication of Survey Center poll releases and materials, including press releases, reports, media advisories, website content, and social media Plan special events and programs in conjunction with Institute team Execute special projects as assigned by the Executive Director Maintain records of Institute traditions, events, policies, operating protocols, reference materials, exhibits, polling history, institutional archives and historical collections Leadership & Staff Supervision Provide direct supervision and performance management of the Program Assistant Oversee work planning, prioritization, and delegation of all Institute administrative responsibilities Coordinate administrative support between the Institute and the Politics Department (as managed by the faculty department chair and the Program Assistant) Oversee student interns and student intern projects with assistance from the Program Assistant Financial Management & Oversight Oversee the Institutes financial planning and forecasting of the annual operating expenses, budget development and monitoring and reporting and analysis Execute and monitor polling budgets, contracts, and consultant payments  Partner with the Finance Office on Institute finances and budgeting Qualifications Bachelor's degree preferred, along with a minimum of seven to ten years of experience in the area of public relations/communications, business and/or politics Highly motivated self-starter who takes initiative and is driven to achieve results Ability to collaborate with campus partners and external agencies and constituencies organizing and implementing Institute events and programs Attention to detail, strong organizational skills and ability to manage multiple projects and deadlines Demonstrated experience in brand management, public relations and strategic communications in a higher education, civic, nonprofit, public policy or political environment.  Supervisory and/or management experience with demonstrated ability to foster a collaborative team environment Enthusiastic, reliable team player with a commitment to excellence in customer service and professionalism Excellent written and interpersonal communication skills Budget experience preferred but not necessary Availability to work occasional evening and weekend hours for special programs or events In order to be considered for this position, please submit both a resume and a cover letter. Successful candidates will be able to assist the college to further its strategic goals for institution-wide diversity and inclusiveness ."
    },
    "https://anselm.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/Anselm/job/Saint-Anselm-College-Campus/Library-Technical-Assistant---Circulation_R1688-1": {
      "title": "Library Technical Assistant - Circulation",
      "description": "Start date: 2026-09-23\nPosted: Posted 22 Days Ago\nPart time\n\nGeisel Library at Saint Anselm College, a private liberal arts college in the Benedictine Catholic tradition, seeks multiple part-time Circulation Desk Supervisors to join their team. Currently shifts are available on Friday and Monday evenings as below during the fall 2026 and spring 2027 semesters with the occasional option of other shifts as needed: Friday 4:00 pm - 11:00 pm Monday 4:00 pm - 12:00 am Interested candidates should complete the online application and be certain to include a Resume, Cover letter, and 3 References.   Job duties The Circulation Supervisor is responsible for supervising all Circulation Desk operations during assigned hours. This person performs a variety of duties including: assisting library users (students, faculty, staff, guests) with a variety of Circulation Desk needs; supervising Circulation Desk student-assistants; monitoring building conditions and security; maintaining adherence to Circulation policies; working on special projects as assigned; and completing daily shift reports, and closing the library each evening and readying the building for use the next day. Additional hours may be possible throughout the year.   Required skills Exceptional level of attendance and dependability, including during inclement weather Exceptional customer service abilities and interpersonal skills Ability to address patron concerns and difficulties with tact, courtesy, and sound judgment Ability to communicate effectively with college students and to supervise student workers Ability to work autonomously and successfully with limited direct daily supervision Strong office and computer skills and the ability to learn new procedures Reliability in performing daily tasks/duties as assigned Ability to consider occasional assistance with coverage of other shifts (optional) Ability to lift 30 lbs.   Required Experience Associate’s degree or 2 years of college plus 1 – 2 years of relevant experience, or the equivalent combination of education, experience, and maturity.    Desired Experience Some supervisory experience Library Circulation Desk experience Experience working with automated library computer systems and/or contemporary interfaces. Successful candidates will be able to assist the college to further its strategic goals for institution-wide diversity and inclusiveness ."
    }
  },
